 ## Abstract
 
-Multi-agent systems must preserve local experience and make reusable knowledge available across tasks and participants. Storage and retrieval, however, do not establish that the knowledge applies to a current decision. This case study examines agent-private notes, project scopes, and shared Team memory in Agent Center. Implementation analysis, Git–event reconciliation, read receipts, and seven purposively selected case groups distinguish persistence, canonical publication, body access, explicit adoption, and local outcomes. Two nonempty Team repositories contain 57 commits corresponding to 30 proposals, 15 promotions, and 12 rejections. The 230 deduplicated read receipts cover 12 agents and 10 rules; nine rules were accessed by multiple agents. The sharing path was used, yet an unconditional main-branch completion rule conflicted with the acceptance order for intermediate candidates. Renaming tasks and editing the graph did not resolve the constraint. A later rule specifying intermediate-node conditions was explicitly adopted in subsequent handoffs that reached local task completion. The findings identify knowledge applicability, publication status, and version relationships as central design concerns beyond persistence and retrieval. In the inspected implementation, project directories organize private knowledge rather than establish a project-wide shared store. Incomplete private histories prevent a comparison of delivery benefits across memory layers. The study supports conditional rules and traceable governance while leaving their independent effects on autonomous delivery unmeasured.
+Multi-agent systems must preserve local experience and make reusable knowledge available across tasks and participants. Storage and retrieval, however, do not establish that the knowledge applies to a current decision. This case study examines how published Team rules in Agent Center can obstruct intermediate handoffs when their conditions do not match the task stage; agent-private notes and project scopes provide implementation context. Implementation analysis, Git–event reconciliation, read receipts, and seven purposively selected case groups distinguish persistence, canonical publication, body access, explicit adoption, and local outcomes. Two nonempty Team repositories contain 57 commits corresponding to 30 proposals, 15 promotions, and 12 rejections. The 230 deduplicated read receipts cover 12 agents and 10 rules; nine rules were accessed by multiple agents. The sharing path was used, yet an unconditional main-branch completion rule conflicted with the acceptance order for intermediate candidates. Renaming tasks and editing the graph did not resolve the constraint. A later rule specifying intermediate-node conditions was explicitly adopted in subsequent handoffs that reached local task completion. The local evidence instantiates established concerns about memory use and updating as rule applicability, publication status, and version relationships; it does not establish a new general memory mechanism. In the inspected implementation, project directories organize private knowledge rather than establish a project-wide shared store. Incomplete private histories prevent a comparison of delivery benefits across memory layers. The study supports conditional rules and traceable governance while leaving their independent effects on autonomous delivery unmeasured.
 
 **Keywords:** agent memory; knowledge governance; context sharing; rule applicability; multi-agent systems
 
